abstract: >-
  An experimental bench testing whether a neural network can learn to produce
  language from a scalar reward alone, with random initial weights, no
  pretraining and no input/output pairs. The search spaces are small enough to
  enumerate exhaustively, so validity mass, effective modes, the Gibbs optimum
  and the conditional structure are computed exactly rather than estimated.
  Test 1 copies a fixed sentence and shows the success comes from reward
  decomposability rather than from search. Test 2 uses a hand-written grammar
  judged by a deterministic parser, reaching 99.9 percent grammaticality with no
  learned rule, and isolates the cause of mode collapse. Version 0.3.0 adds a
  closed-form bound: a policy with no determiner-to-noun coupling has a product
  support, so at full validity it is capped by the largest fully valid product
  in the sublanguage it enters. That bound is never exceeded in 70 runs, is
  attained exactly by half of them, and is a plateau rather than a basin.
  Version 0.4.0 adds the reversal test: a three-gender lexicon puts the two
  ceilings in a ratio of 3 instead of 2, which no relabelling can produce, and
  the observed maxima follow the prediction exactly. It also switches the
  advantage line to a single rounding, which turns out to be a better
  controlled experiment than a seed, since it perturbs the trajectory while
  leaving the initialisation bit-identical: 70 of 70 seeds keep their corner
  while only 21 of 70 keep their mode count.

  Acknowledgment. The product bound is due to Dipankar Sarkar
  (ORCID 0000-0001-5431-6367), who derived the order-1 marginals independently
  from the published article and then showed that the two degenerate corners
  hold the same number of valid sentences but not the same largest product.
  Three rounds of his criticism also corrected a statistic that measured
  determiner coverage rather than agreement, a saturation metric that could
  exceed 100 percent, and a sample-size claim that pooled three seeds into
  twenty-four rows.
keywords:
  - reinforcement learning
  - REINFORCE
  - mode collapse
  - emergent communication
  - reward shaping
  - grammar
